_🛠️ Refactor suggestion_

**Consider scoping the keyboard listener to avoid unintended triggers.**

The global `window` event listener will trigger the `generate` function regardless of user focus, which could cause unexpected behavior when multiple query nodes exist or when users are interacting with other parts of the application.



Consider scoping the event listener to a specific container or checking if the query node is currently focused:

```diff
 	useEffect(() => {
+		let containerRef: HTMLElement | null = null;
+		
 		const handleKeyDown = (event: KeyboardEvent) => {
+			// Only trigger if the query node properties panel is focused
+			if (!containerRef?.contains(document.activeElement)) {
+				return;
+			}
+			
 			const isMac = /Mac|iPod|iPhone|iPad/.test(navigator.platform);
 			if (
 				event.key === "Enter" &&
 				((isMac && event.metaKey) || (!isMac && event.ctrlKey))
 			) {
 				generate();
 			}
 		};
-		window.addEventListener("keydown", handleKeyDown);
-		return () => window.removeEventListener("keydown", handleKeyDown);
+		
+		// Get reference to the properties panel container
+		containerRef = document.querySelector('[data-query-node-properties]');
+		
+		if (containerRef) {
+			containerRef.addEventListener("keydown", handleKeyDown);
+			return () => containerRef?.removeEventListener("keydown", handleKeyDown);
+		}
 	}, [generate]);
```

Or alternatively, accept a `ref` prop to scope the listener to a specific element.


<!-- suggestion_start -->

<details>
<summary>📝 Committable suggestion</summary>

> ‼️ **IMPORTANT**
> Carefully review the code before committing. Ensure that it accurately replaces the highlighted code, contains no missing lines, and has no issues with indentation. Thoroughly test & benchmark the code to ensure it meets the requirements.

```suggestion
    useEffect(() => {
        let containerRef: HTMLElement | null = null;

        const handleKeyDown = (event: KeyboardEvent) => {
            // Only trigger if the query node properties panel is focused
            if (!containerRef?.contains(document.activeElement)) {
                return;
            }

            const isMac = /Mac|iPod|iPhone|iPad/.test(navigator.platform);
            if (
                event.key === "Enter" &&
                ((isMac && event.metaKey) || (!isMac && event.ctrlKey))
            ) {
                generate();
            }
        };

        // Get reference to the properties panel container
        containerRef = document.querySelector('[data-query-node-properties]');

        if (containerRef) {
            containerRef.addEventListener("keydown", handleKeyDown);
            return () => containerRef.removeEventListener("keydown", handleKeyDown);
        }
    }, [generate]);
```

</details>

<!-- suggestion_end -->

<details>
<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>

```
In
internal-packages/workflow-designer-ui/src/editor/properties-panel/query-node-properties-panel/keyboard-shortcuts.tsx
around lines 8 to 20, the global window keydown event listener triggers the
generate function regardless of user focus, causing unintended behavior. To fix
this, scope the event listener by either attaching it to a specific container
element or by checking if the relevant query node is currently focused before
calling generate. Alternatively, modify the component to accept a ref prop and
add the event listener only to the referenced element to limit the scope of the
keyboard shortcut.
```

</details>

