body:
![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

Creating a `setTimeout` for every single request can introduce performance overhead under high load by adding many timers to the event loop.

A more performant approach would be to clear the timer if the request finishes before the threshold is met. You can do this by clearing the timeout inside the `res.on('finish', ...)` event handler.

Example:
```typescript
const slowRequestTimer = setTimeout(() => {
  // ... slow request logging logic
}, slowRequestThreshold);

res.on('finish', () => {
  clearTimeout(slowRequestTimer);
  // ... other finish logic
});
```

