body:
![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

This implementation for waiting on user input sets the terminal to raw mode but doesn't handle termination signals like `Ctrl-C`. If the user cancels the operation while the script is waiting for input, the process will exit, but the terminal might be left in a raw state, which can be disruptive for the user.

To make this more robust, you should also listen for the `Ctrl-C` key press (which sends character code `3`) and ensure that the terminal state is cleaned up before exiting.

```typescript
// Check for Enter key (code 13) or Ctrl-C (code 3)
        if (key[0] === 13 || key[0] === 3) {
          process.stdin.setRawMode(false);
          process.stdin.pause();
          process.stdin.removeListener('data', handler);

          if (key[0] === 3) {
            console.log('\nOperation cancelled by user.');
            process.exit(130); // Standard exit code for SIGINT
          }
          resolve();
        }
```

