body:
![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

This correctly implements the responsive border-radius for the badge. However, it introduces a potential maintainability issue by duplicating the breakpoint values (`480px`, `360px`) and radius values (`20px`, `16px`) that are likely already defined for the `.next-shift-card`.

If the card's responsive styles are ever updated, the badge styles will also need to be updated manually, which is error-prone.

A more robust solution would be to use CSS Custom Properties (variables) on the parent `.next-shift-card` to define the radius, and then have the badge inherit that value.

For example (this would require changes to `.next-shift-card` styles):
```css
/* On the card */
.next-shift-card {
  --card-corner-radius: 24px;
  border-radius: var(--card-corner-radius);
}
@media (max-width: 480px) {
  .next-shift-card { --card-corner-radius: 20px; }
}
@media (max-width: 360px) {
  .next-shift-card { --card-corner-radius: 16px; }
}

/* Then the badge style becomes much simpler and more maintainable */
.next-shift-badge {
  border-radius: 0 var(--card-corner-radius) var(--card-corner-radius) 0;
}
```

Since modifying `.next-shift-card` might be out of scope for this PR, this is a suggestion for a future refactoring to improve maintainability. The current implementation is functionally correct.

