body:
![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

Directly setting `innerHTML` by concatenating strings that include `loc.name` and `loc.description` poses a significant Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) risk. If `loc.name` or `loc.description` were to contain malicious HTML or JavaScript (e.g., from user input or an external data source in the future), it would be executed. 

Could this be refactored to use safer methods, such as setting `textContent` for textual data and creating/appending DOM elements if HTML structure is needed? 

For example:
```javascript
infoContent.textContent = ''; // Clear existing content first

const nameElement = document.createElement('h2');
nameElement.textContent = loc.name;
infoContent.appendChild(nameElement);

const descriptionElement = document.createElement('p');
descriptionElement.textContent = loc.description;
infoContent.appendChild(descriptionElement);
```

```javascript
      infoContent.textContent = ''; // Clear existing content
      const nameHeader = document.createElement('h2');
      nameHeader.textContent = loc.name; // Safely sets text, preventing XSS
      infoContent.appendChild(nameHeader);
      const descPara = document.createElement('p');
      descPara.textContent = loc.description; // Safely sets text, preventing XSS
      infoContent.appendChild(descPara);
```

